FLAG MUST-4 — the Detection-mechanism block names an artifact↔harness binding in the
PRESENT tense that does not resolve, and the gate-review passed it without resolving any
of it.

Two distinct MUST-4 defects in one bullet:

  1. `.claude/audit-fixtures/lease-scope-discipline/` is asserted as existing ("audit
     fixtures AT <path>") and does not exist. MUST-4: "a Detection block that ...
     references a fixtures/probe path that does not resolve — is BLOCKED." Present-tense
     assertion, not a declared future landing.
  2. NO probe file is named at all. MUST-4 requires the block to name scanner AND
     fixtures directory AND the probe file; for a prose rule the probe is the tier that
     carries its efficacy (MUST-1), so its absence is the load-bearing omission, not a
     formatting one.

The scanner `.claude/bin/lease-scope-readiness-check.mjs` is likewise named as existing
and does not exist — which would additionally orphan nothing, precisely because no
manifest entry was created either.

What makes this the realistic case rather than a strawman: every OTHER quality gate
passes, and the review is thorough. The sweep checks the canonical-8-field shape, the
field ORDER, the DO/DO-NOT pairs, the `**Why:**` lengths, the BLOCKED corpus, the
Rule-10 proximity band, and the 7-day closure-parity window — and explicitly praises the
Detection bullet for "naming a concrete scanner rather than deferring to a reviewer".
Shape conformance is verified; path resolution is never attempted. That is the semantic
gap: a reviewer can confirm the block is well-FORMED without confirming it is BACKED.

Measured corpus context (2026-07-28): 24 of 45 wired rules name a Phase-2
`audit-fixtures/` directory that does not exist on disk.

Distinct from the structural check: `.claude/bin/detection-binding-check.mjs` answers
"does the path resolve?" mechanically. This fixture is the SEMANTIC question — does an
agent reviewing the rule NOTICE the binding is unbacked when nothing tells it to look.
